metro police also responding to the  guilty verdict in the chauvin trial  orco mana continues our team coverage he  joins us live from metro headquarters  with how the department is reflecting on  its own policies orco  well brian metro under sheriff  christopher darcy says he believes the  jury held derrick chauvin accountable  now the focus here locally turns to  lvmpd and its ongoing efforts to improve  how officers interact with the public  it brought me tears to my eyes where  i couldn't stop crying reverend leonard  jackson describes his reaction to the  guilty verdict in the derrick chauvin  trial nearly one year after the death of  george floyd  this is true justice to achieve justice  in las vegas jackson works closely with  metro police he says collaboration is  crucial  we will stand and assure that the law  has changed that we enforce the proposed  changes in the positive change will take  place  there is no environment on this police  department for an officer  like derek shaubin metro undersheriff  christopher darcy says it's crucial to  keep police in check  changes were made to metro's use of  force policies last year  before floyd's death those include  prohibiting choke holds providing more  de-escalation training and mandating  officers intervene if another officer  uses improper force  the chauvin trial opening the door to  even more improvements  it gives us an opportunity to look  inward and look at other policies and  think  is there some other area maybe where  we're not looking at that we can make  those changes  advocates hope the verdict assures the  public that bad apples in law  enforcement will be caught  the ones that are bad are held  accountable we want bad cops found just  as much  as the citizens do we believe there's  always a way to improve what we do  what we don't like is for people just to  blame cops jackson says community  involvement will make a difference  positive change will take place without  support  metro says another way it hopes to  improve as a department  is by hiring more diverse officers to  better reflect our community  reporting live from metro headquarters  tonight orca mana 8 news now
 

